        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "**Roster countries:** Yazaki — JPN, Sumitomo Electric — JPN, Aptiv — USA.\n\n**Same duty treatment:** On roster country alone, Yazaki and Sumitomo Electric group together (both JPN); Aptiv sits apart (USA). But that's a grouping, not a duty answer — the roster doesn't tell you what rate applies to either group.\n\n**What the roster country doesn't tell you:** essentially everything that actually drives the duty.\n\n- It's a corporate/roster country, not a country of origin. Wire harnesses are labour-intensive and typically assembled well away from headquarters — Mexico, North Africa, Eastern Europe, Southeast Asia are the usual sites for all three of these firms. A JPN roster entry does not mean the harness ships from Japan, and Aptiv's USA entry does not mean domestic manufacture.\n- No plant locations, no shipping origin, no substantial-transformation analysis, no component sourcing.\n- No tariff classification, no preference-programme eligibility (USMCA and similar rules could matter a great deal here), and no rates.\n\nSo the honest answer: this data can sort the three suppliers into two buckets by recorded country, and nothing more. To say who faces the same import duty you'd need the origin and shipping point of the specific harnesses being bid, plus the HTS classification — none of which is in the roster."
          }
        ],
